{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Impact update, measured 18:49 UTC — the alarm surface has degraded further, and it is now overwhelmingly false.\n\n**19 alarms in ALARM.** The metrics behind them:\n\n    data_disk_debris_bytes            journal_cap_in_effect            review_bot_merge_change_errors\n    disk_used_percent                 journal_used_percent             review_interrupts_bound_exceeded\n    docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent  mcp_mem_abort                  review_interrupts_signal_unavailable\n    docker_storage_used_percent       mcp_retire_cap                   var_tmp_cleanup_active\n    docker_unaccounted_bytes          mirror_out_of_sync               var_tmp_used_percent\n    g2p_dispatch_errors               replication_errors               voter_errors\n    gate_scratch_mounted\n\n**Newest datapoint and value for a representative sample:**\n\n    root_disk_used_percent   18:44Z (5 min ago)   = 73\n    mem_used_percent         18:39Z (10 min ago)  = 65\n    voter_errors             18:39Z (10 min ago)  = 0\n    replication_errors       18:39Z (10 min ago)  = 0\n    journal_used_percent     18:39Z (10 min ago)  = 57\n    gerrit_healthy           NO DATA in 4 hours\n\nSo the metrics are publishing, recently, with **healthy values** — zero errors, journal at 57%, memory at 65%, root at 73%. Only two of the nineteen correspond to a real condition: `gate_scratch_mounted` (0, the unmounted volume — `dcc3`) and `var_tmp_cleanup_active` (0, awaiting the `rootflags=pquota` reboot). Both are known and operator-pending.\n\n**Roughly 17 of 19 alarms are reporting a condition that is not true.** Earlier today the same count was 4 of 6. The ratio is getting worse as the probe is killed earlier in its run.\n\n**A second observation that supports the memory-pressure link, and is worth someone testing properly.** `mem_used_percent` stalled completely between 17:40Z and 18:39Z — 49 minutes with no datapoint. It resumed after I restarted the MCP container at ~18:32, which took host memory from 83% used / 990 MB available to 4,314 MB available. That is consistent with the probe getting further through its run when the host is not thrashing, i.e. memory pressure aggravates the I/O stall rather than causing it. One observation, one intervention — suggestive, not established, and the per-section timing this ticket asks for would settle it.\n\n**Why the ratio matters more than the count.** An operator facing 19 alarms of which 17 are false cannot triage by reading the alarm names; they have to go to the datapoints for every one, which is what the alarm surface exists to spare them. I did exactly that three times today and twice nearly drew the wrong conclusion — once treating a cluster of five as a submit-triggered incident, once reading a firing BuildKit alarm as evidence of a real overshoot when it had been red on missing data since the previous evening."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788634197663942069,"uuid":"b4cc1f15-3a27-4752-a7e3-1b443f3f4630"}